Multi Variable Calculus


Lec 1


Vectors
 
Vector Definition
 
\vec{A} = \langle a_1, a_2, a_3 \rangle
Magnitude:
|\vec{A}| = \sqrt{a_1^2 + a_2^2 + a_3^2}
 
 
 
Vector Addition
 
\vec{A} + \vec{B} = \langle a_1+b_1, a_2+b_2, a_3+b_3 \rangle
 
 
 
DOT PRODUCT
 
Formula:
\vec{A} \cdot \vec{B} = |\vec{A}||\vec{B}|\cos(\theta)
 
Definition:
\vec{A} \cdot \vec{B} = \sum a_i b_i = a_1b_1 + a_2b_2 + a_3b_3
(This result is a SCALAR!)
 
Geometric Interpretation:
\vec{A} \cdot \vec{B} = |\vec{A}| \cdot |\vec{B}| \cos(\theta)
where \theta is the angle between vectors \vec{A} and \vec{B}.
 
 
 
Law of Cosines
 
For \vec{C} = \vec{A} - \vec{B}:
|\vec{C}|^2 = |\vec{A}|^2 + |\vec{B}|^2 - 2|\vec{A}||\vec{B}|\cos\theta
From this, we derive:
\vec{A} \cdot \vec{B} = |\vec{A}| \cdot |\vec{B}| \cos\theta
\cos\theta = \frac{\vec{A} \cdot \vec{B}}{|\vec{A}| \cdot |\vec{B}|}
 
 
 
Orthogonality Condition
 
\vec{A} \cdot \vec{B} = 0 \quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad \cos\theta = 0
\quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad \theta = 90^\circ
\quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad \vec{A} \perp \vec{B}
 
Sign of the Dot Product
 
$$
\vec{A} \cdot \vec{B}
\begin{cases}
 
0 & \text{if } \theta < 90^\circ \
= 0 & \text{if } \theta = 90^\circ \
< 0 & \text{if } \theta > 90^\circ

\end{cases}
$$



Lec 2
 
 
 
Review: Dot Product
 
\vec{A} \cdot \vec{B} = \sum a_i b_i = |\vec{A}||\vec{B}|\cos\theta
 
 
 
Applications of the Dot Product
 
1. Find lengths & angles
\cos\theta = \frac{\vec{A} \cdot \vec{B}}{|\vec{A}||\vec{B}|}
​
2. Detect orthogonality
\vec{A} \perp \vec{B} \quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad \vec{A} \cdot \vec{B} = 0
(Note: \hat{u} is a unit vector, so |\hat{u}|=1)
​
3. Components of \vec{A} along direction \hat{u}
The scalar component of \vec{A} along \hat{u} is:
|\vec{A}|\cos(\theta) = |\vec{A}||\hat{u}|\cos\theta = \vec{A} \cdot \hat{u}
 
 
 
Related Concepts
 
- Work done by a force:
W = F \cdot s \cdot \cos\theta
​
- Area of a triangle (formed by vectors \vec{A} and \vec{B}):
\text{Area} = \frac{1}{2}|\vec{A}||\vec{B}|\sin\theta
(To find \sin\theta, first compute \cos\theta from the dot product, then use \sin^2\theta + \cos^2\theta = 1)
 
 
 
2D Perpendicular Vectors & Determinants
 
- For a vector \vec{A} = \langle a_1, a_2 \rangle, a perpendicular vector is \vec{A}' = \langle -a_2, a_1 \rangle, so \vec{A} \perp \vec{A}'.
​
- The magnitude |\vec{A}||\vec{B}|\sin\theta (which relates to the area of the parallelogram formed by \vec{A} and \vec{B}) can be expressed as:
|\vec{A}||\vec{B}|\sin\theta = \vec{A}' \cdot \vec{B} = \langle -a_2, a_1 \rangle \cdot \langle b_1, b_2 \rangle = a_1b_2 - a_2b_1
​
- This value is the determinant of the matrix formed by \vec{A} and \vec{B}:
\det(\vec{A}, \vec{B}) = \begin{vmatrix} a_1 & a_2 \\ b_1 & b_2 \end{vmatrix} = a_1b_2 - a_2b_1
It represents the area of the parallelogram spanned by vectors \vec{A} and \vec{B}.

Determinant & Area
 
For vectors \vec{A} and \vec{B} in 2D:
 
- The area of the parallelogram they span is:
\pm \text{Area}_{\square} = |\vec{A}||\vec{B}|\sin\theta = \det(\vec{A}, \vec{B})
​
- The area of the triangle they form is:
\pm \text{Area}_{\triangle} = \frac{1}{2}|\vec{A}||\vec{B}|\sin\theta = \frac{1}{2}\det(\vec{A}, \vec{B})
 
 
 
Determinant in 3D Space
 
For three vectors \vec{A} = \langle a_1, a_2, a_3 \rangle, \vec{B} = \langle b_1, b_2, b_3 \rangle, \vec{C} = \langle c_1, c_2, c_3 \rangle:

\det(\vec{A}, \vec{B}, \vec{C}) =
\begin{vmatrix}
a_1 & a_2 & a_3 \\
b_1 & b_2 & b_3 \\
c_1 & c_2 & c_3
\end{vmatrix}

Expanding along the first row:
$$
= a_1
\begin{vmatrix} b_2 & b_3 \ c_2 & c_3 \end{vmatrix}
 
- a_2
\begin{vmatrix} b_1 & b_3 \ c_1 & c_3 \end{vmatrix}
 
- a_3
\begin{vmatrix} b_1 & b_2 \ c_1 & c_2 \end{vmatrix}
$$
 
Theorem (Geometric Interpretation):
\det(\vec{A}, \vec{B}, \vec{C}) = \pm \text{Volume of the parallelepiped spanned by } \vec{A}, \vec{B}, \vec{C}
 
 
 
Cross Product of 2 Vectors in 3D Space
 
Definition:
The cross product \vec{A} \times \vec{B} results in a vector that is perpendicular to both \vec{A} and \vec{B}.

\vec{A} \times \vec{B} =
\begin{vmatrix}
\hat{i} & \hat{j} & \hat{k} \\
a_1 & a_2 & a_3 \\
b_1 & b_2 & b_3
\end{vmatrix}

Expanding the determinant:

=
\begin{vmatrix} a_2 & a_3 \\ b_2 & b_3 \end{vmatrix} \hat{i}
-
\begin{vmatrix} a_1 & a_3 \\ b_1 & b_3 \end{vmatrix} \hat{j}
+
\begin{vmatrix} a_1 & a_2 \\ b_1 & b_2 \end{vmatrix} \hat{k}

Cross Product Magnitude & Direction
 
Theorem:
The magnitude of the cross product equals the area of the parallelogram spanned by \vec{A} and \vec{B}:
|\vec{A} \times \vec{B}| = \text{Area of parallelogram}
 
The direction of \vec{A} \times \vec{B} is perpendicular to the plane containing \vec{A} and \vec{B}.
To determine the exact direction, use the Right-Hand Rule:
 
1. Point your right hand fingers along \vec{A}.
​
2. Curl your fingers towards \vec{B}.
​
3. Your thumb points in the direction of \vec{A} \times \vec{B}.
 
 
 
Anti-commutativity of Cross Product
 
\vec{A} \times \vec{B} = - \vec{B} \times \vec{A}
 
 
 
Volume of a Parallelepiped (Revisited)
 
The volume can be expressed as:
\text{Volume} = \text{Area of base} \times \text{height}
Using the cross product and dot product:
\text{Volume} = |\vec{B} \times \vec{C}| \times (\vec{A} \cdot \hat{n})
where \hat{n} = \frac{\vec{B} \times \vec{C}}{|\vec{B} \times \vec{C}|} is the unit normal vector to the base.
 
This simplifies to the scalar triple product, which is equivalent to the determinant:
\det(\vec{A}, \vec{B}, \vec{C}) = \vec{A} \cdot (\vec{B} \times \vec{C})
 
 
 
Cross Product Magnitude Formula
 
|\vec{A} \times \vec{B}| = |\vec{A}||\vec{B}|\sin\theta

Lec 3: Matrices
 
 
 
Matrix Multiplication
 
- Dimension Rule: For matrices A and B, the number of columns in A must equal the number of rows in B.
If A is a (3 \times 4) matrix and B is a (4 \times 2) matrix, then AB is a (3 \times 2) matrix.
​
- Interpretation as Transformations:
The product AB represents applying transformation B first, then transformation A.
(AB)\vec{x} = A(B\vec{x})
​
- Important Note:
Matrix multiplication is not commutative:
AB \neq BA
 
 
 
Identity Matrix
 
The identity matrix I acts like the number 1 for matrices, satisfying:
I\vec{x} = \vec{x}
 
- General form for an n \times n identity matrix:
I_{n \times n} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & & 0 \\ & \ddots & \\ 0 & & 1 \end{bmatrix}
​
- Example:
I_{3 \times 3} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}
 
Example: Rotation Matrix
 
A 90^\circ counter-clockwise rotation in the plane is given by:
R = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & -1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{bmatrix}
Applying it twice results in a 180^\circ rotation:
R^2 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 & 0 \\ 0 & -1 \end{bmatrix} = -I_{2 \times 2}
 
 
 
Inverse Matrix
 
- The inverse of a matrix A, denoted A^{-1}, is the unique matrix such that:
AA^{-1} = I \quad \text{and} \quad A^{-1}A = I
​
- Solving Linear Systems:
For the equation A\vec{x} = \vec{B}, the solution is:
\vec{x} = A^{-1}\vec{B}
​
- Formula for Inverse:
A^{-1} = \frac{1}{\det(A)} \text{adj}(A)
where \text{adj}(A) is the adjoint matrix of A.

Inverse Matrix (Step-by-Step)
 
Inverse Matrix Formula
 
A^{-1} = \frac{1}{\det(A)} \text{adj}(A)
where \text{adj}(A) is the adjoint matrix.
 
 
 
Step-by-Step Calculation (3×3 Example)
 
For the matrix:
A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 & 3 & 3 \\ 2 & 4 & 5 \\ 1 & 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}
 
1. Calculate Minors
 
For each entry, compute the determinant of the submatrix obtained by removing its row and column.

\text{Minors} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & -1 & -2 \\ 3 & 1 & -1 \\ 3 & 4 & 2 \end{pmatrix}

 
2. Calculate Cofactors
 
Apply a "checkerboard" sign pattern to the minors:
$$
\begin{bmatrix}
 
- & - & + \
 
- & + & - \
 
- & - & +
\end{bmatrix}

\text{Cofactors} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & +1 & -2 \ -3 & 1 & +1 \ 3 & -4 & 2 \end{pmatrix}
$$
 
3. Transpose to get the Adjoint Matrix
 
Transpose the cofactor matrix (swap rows and columns):

\text{adj}(A) = \begin{bmatrix} 3 & -3 & 3 \\ 1 & 1 & -4 \\ -2 & 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}

 
4. Compute the Determinant of A
 

\det(A) = \begin{vmatrix} 2 & 3 & 3 \\ 2 & 4 & 5 \\ 1 & 1 & 2 \end{vmatrix} = 3

 
5. Divide Adjoint by Determinant
 

A^{-1} = \frac{1}{3} \begin{bmatrix} 3 & -3 & 3 \\ 1 & 1 & -4 \\ -2 & 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix}



Lec 4: 
Invertibility & Solutions to Linear Systems
 
 
 
Inverse Matrix Formula
 
A^{-1} = \frac{1}{\det(A)} \text{adj}(A)
 
Invertibility Condition
 
A matrix A is invertible if and only if its determinant is not zero:
A \text{ is invertible} \quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad \det(A) \neq 0
 
 
 
General Solution to A\vec{x} = \vec{B}
 
The existence and uniqueness of the solution depend on the determinant of A:
 
1. If \det(A) \neq 0
The matrix is invertible, so there is a unique solution:
\vec{x} = A^{-1}\vec{B}
​
2. If \det(A) = 0
The matrix is singular, so there is either no solution or infinitely many solutions.
